  <h1>How to get a document notarized after hours or on a weekend</h1>
  <p class="meta">Updated {{ updated | displayDate }}</p>
  <p>Banks and shipping stores often stop notarizing in the late afternoon, including on days when you only got the document at the end of the workday. A hospital, a closing, or a Sunday flight can make the timing worse. You still have three realistic paths: a notary who publishes evening hours, a mobile notary who will come to you, or a remote online session when the recipient accepts one.</p>
  <p>LateNotary does not notarize documents. The city pages linked below are a directory of businesses that publish those options. Confirm the slot and the total fee with the business before anyone travels.</p>

  <h2>Ask the recipient before you book</h2>
  <p>The person or office who will receive the document decides what they will accept. Ask two questions:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Do you need an in-person, wet-ink notarization?</li>
    <li>Will you accept remote online notarization, which is a recorded video session instead of a stamp you can see on the page?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Some banks, courts, employers, and foreign authorities still refuse an online notarization even when your state allows it. If they require ink, skip the online option and book an in-person notary. If they accept online, that can be the fallback when nobody can reach you tonight.</p>

  <h2>What to have ready</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A current government-issued photo ID for every signer. The name on the ID should match the document.</li>
    <li>The complete document, with blanks filled in. Leave signatures unsigned unless the notary tells you otherwise. Signing too early is a common reason a notarization cannot be completed.</li>
    <li>Every signer present and willing. A notary cannot notarize a signature for someone who is not there, and cannot take a signer’s word that another person already signed.</li>
    <li>A quiet spot for a mobile visit, or a charged phone and a stable connection if the session is online.</li>
    <li>If the document needs witnesses, ask the notary whether they can bring impartial witnesses and what that costs. Do not assume friends or family will qualify.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Find a notary open late, a Sunday notary, or a mobile notary</h2>
  <p>On a city page, the Evening filter shows businesses that publish hours at or after 6:00 PM or that advertise after-hours appointments. Saturday and Sunday filters show weekend availability. Mobile means the notary travels to a home, office, lobby, hotel, or other place they list. Flags come from the business’s own pages. They are not a promise that someone is free in the next hour.</p>
  <p>Call or text two or three notaries. Say where you are, how many signers and documents you have, whether anyone is in a hospital or jail, and the latest time you can meet. Get an arrival window before you wait outside a closed bank branch.</p>
