  <h2>How a mobile or weekend visit usually works</h2>
  <p>A mobile notary comes to you. That is the practical meaning of a search like “mobile notary near me”: the visit happens at your address, not at a counter that closed at 4 p.m. Weekend and late-evening visits are often by appointment, and the notary may already be with another signer, so the first person you call may not be the one who can arrive.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Confirm the address, parking or front-desk rules, and who will meet them.</li>
    <li>Ask whether the time you need is inside their published hours. A Sunday notary who lists afternoon hours may not be available at night, and an evening surcharge does not always mean a visit is actually open.</li>
    <li>If the signing is at a hospital, nursing home, courthouse, or jail, say so when you call. Some businesses publish those visits and some do not. LateNotary only flags them when the business’s own page says so.</li>
    <li>When the notary arrives, sign only when they tell you to, and check that your name and the document title are correct before they seal it.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Typical fees</h2>
  <p>States often set a small maximum for the notarial act itself. Mobile notaries also charge for travel and time. That travel fee is usually the larger part of the bill. Sunday, late-evening, holiday, hospital, jail, and rush visits often cost more than a standard weekday appointment.</p>
  <p>When a business publishes a price, the city page copies it into that listing’s fee notes with the date it was checked. If no price was published, the listing says so and tells you to ask. Get the total, including travel, extra signatures, witnesses, and any after-hours surcharge, before anyone starts driving. LateNotary does not set those prices and does not take a cut of a local notary’s fee.</p>

